# Summary
Introduced: the new drain-the-whole-page scheduling policy causes prepared artifacts from successive four-worker waves to accumulate in `prepared_for_authorization` until all work from the 16-PG page has completed. The commit adds no corresponding aggregate memory limit.
The changed scheduling order dispatches pending transfers and returns until both the pending queue and all in-flight work are empty. Completed preparation jobs are meanwhile appended to `prepared_for_authorization`, and authorization—which consumes those owners—does not run until the whole selected page has drained. A scan page can contain 16 PGs, while the transfer pool has only four workers, so the new behavior intentionally accumulates multiple worker waves in memory.

Each `PreparedUnavailablePgMetadataTransfer` retains both a boxed decoded `PreparedLivePgMetadataTransfer` and a separate `staged_artifact: Vec<u8>`. An individual encoded artifact is allowed to be as large as 63 MiB. Consequently, one full page can retain 16 × 63 MiB = 1008 MiB of encoded data alone, in addition to the decoded checkpoints/log entries, worker-local encoding buffers, and normal process memory. There is no aggregate byte budget before the authorization stage; queue byte metrics are observational only.

An authenticated S3 client can grow the metadata checkpoints and retained command logs by creating large numbers of objects and associated metadata across the configured PGs. If those PGs subsequently enter unavailable-PG reconciliation—for example after a storage-node lease expiry or a node outage—the retained artifacts can drive the process into OOM. The state remains present after restart, so reconciliation can reproduce the allocation and create a crash loop. Attack complexity is high because the attacker must groom metadata across multiple PGs and cause or wait for a placement transition, but no privileged control-plane access is needed to create the metadata.

Before this commit, when a four-worker preparation wave completed, the worker authorized that wave before dispatching the next pending wave. The new drain-before-advance ordering increases the encoded-artifact retention bound from approximately one worker wave to the full 16-member page. The batching should enforce an aggregate prepared-artifact byte limit, authorize bounded sub-batches, or spill encoded artifacts to bounded on-disk storage rather than retaining an entire page in memory.

# Validation
## Rubric
- [x] Confirm production scheduling can select 16 PGs, uses four workers, and defers authorization until pending and in-flight work fully drain.
- [x] Confirm every completed prepared transfer remains concurrently owned and retains decoded transfer state plus a separate encoded artifact.
- [x] Confirm a valid artifact can be as large as 63 MiB and no aggregate prepared-queue byte budget limits the 16-member page.
- [x] Confirm authenticated S3-created object/multipart metadata feeds checkpoints and reconciliation is production-reachable after unavailable-node grace expiry.
- [x] Dynamically exercise the closest real harness and demonstrate retention beyond one worker wave, reaching all 16 page members before authorization.
## Report
Confirmed the candidate’s core vulnerability chain at commit 302d6b80981d657a2afa8c07af05117a945f734a.

Attacker input and preconditions: An authenticated S3 client can create many objects, versions, multipart records, tags, and metadata across configured PGs. The canonical metadata checkpoint includes all rows from object and multipart tables, including object keys, tags, metadata blobs, parts, and segments (`crates/storage/src/pg_store/command_log.rs:567-889`, especially the `objects` table at 811-847), and checkpoint construction materializes those table blocks (`command_log.rs:4448-4523`). Per-object user metadata is limited to 2 KiB (`crates/server-core/src/metadata_blob.rs:19-23`), but there is no aggregate per-PG metadata-size quota in this path, so many ordinary records can grow a transfer artifact. Exploitation additionally requires up to 16 groomed PGs to become reconciliation candidates in one scan page, typically after a storage-node observation exceeds the configured replacement grace (`crates/storage/src/control_plane.rs:2737-2777`). The client does not directly control lease expiry, so this remains a high-complexity precondition.

Scheduling/control evidence: The production transfer pool has four workers (`crates/storage/src/unavailable_pg_reconciliation.rs:29-30`, `459-524`), while the scan page and transition batch limits are 16 (`crates/storage/src/control_plane.rs:108-115`). Batch scanning can return candidates from all 16 examined PGs (`control_plane.rs:2690-2735`). Completed preparation results are appended to `prepared_for_authorization` (`unavailable_pg_reconciliation.rs:824-855`). At `unavailable_pg_reconciliation.rs:1371-1385`, the changed scheduler dispatches further pending work and returns while either pending or in-flight work exists; it reaches authorization only after the whole page drains. Authorization then takes the complete retained vector with `std::mem::take` (`unavailable_pg_reconciliation.rs:1035-1046`). Comparing the parent with `git show HEAD^:crates/storage/src/unavailable_pg_reconciliation.rs` shows the prior code authorized whenever `in_flight` became empty and withheld further dispatch while prepared owners existed, bounding normal retention to approximately one four-worker wave. Thus the page-wide behavior is introduced by this commit.

Dynamic evidence: The repository’s existing composed live-transfer test was temporarily instrumented and expanded from five to 16 PGs. It uses real control-plane transitions, lease expiry, storage metadata export, `LivePgMetadataTransferAdmin`, and the production four-worker reconciliation worker. Calling `observe_transfer_workers()` received completions without executing authorization, allowing inspection at the exact stage boundary. Command: `cargo test -p storage reconciliation_accumulates_more_pgs_than_transfer_workers_into_one_install -- --nocapture`. It passed and printed `VALIDATION prepared_for_authorization depth=16 artifact_bytes=5248`; assertions also established that both pending and in-flight depths were zero. This directly demonstrates that all 16 real prepared owners from four successive worker waves coexist before authorization. The fixture artifacts were deliberately small; the test did not intentionally consume 1 GiB or crash the runner.

Memory sink and bound: Each prepared owner simultaneously retains a boxed decoded `PreparedLivePgMetadataTransfer` and a separate encoded `staged_artifact: Vec<u8>` (`crates/storage/src/live_pg_transfer.rs:334-342`). Preparation first retains the decoded artifact, separately encodes it, and returns both (`live_pg_transfer.rs:1248-1319`). Encoding rejects only lengths greater than 63 MiB (`crates/storage/src/pg_store/metadata_transfer_staging.rs:528-593`), with the protocol maximum defined as `63 * 1024 * 1024` (`metadata_transfer_staging.rs:64-67`). Therefore the encoded-only page bound is 16 × 63 MiB = 1008 MiB (1,056,964,608 bytes), excluding decoded checkpoints/log entries, temporary encoding/RPC buffers, and ordinary service memory. The worker only sums queue bytes for observability (`unavailable_pg_reconciliation.rs:581-610`); no aggregate prepared-queue byte budget or backpressure is enforced. The destination staging store’s later 4 GiB disk-oriented limit (`crates/storage/src/storage_node_server/server.rs:4-5`, `934-942`) does not bound this pre-authorization in-memory queue.

Impact assessment: On a process with insufficient headroom, valid near-limit artifacts can exhaust memory and terminate the service. Durable object metadata/checkpoints and unavailable-PG control-plane transitions support regeneration after restart, but an actual allocator OOM and repeated crash loop were not executed. The precise amount of decoded-state duplication and the memory threshold at which a particular deployment dies remain environment-dependent. These are impact-quantification gaps rather than counterevidence against the confirmed page-wide accumulation and missing aggregate control.

Artifacts contain the final passing test output, temporary instrumentation patch, bound calculation, and source-reference summary. The instrumentation was removed afterward and the checkout was restored cleanly.

